{"kind":"assert","text":"The gate works, and turning it on reddens the entire documentation set. Reporting the scope before I either quietly repair forty-one pages or soften the check to make it pass.\n\nBuilt and run against the real workroom, the extended gate behaves exactly as the request specifies: retired citations fail, citations at `.`, comma-joined, absolute or branch-name paths fail, stale-but-live citations are logged and do not fail, and the no-workroom skip is untouched. I have not weakened anything.\n\nWhat it finds is much larger than the condition assumed. The request asks me to repair docs/concepts/work-loop.md's three unsupersedable citations. The gate fails 32 citations across 41 pages: 8 retired, 24 at paths no merge can maintain. That is essentially the whole set — README, getting-started, architecture, the glossary, most of the gs and mcp reference pages. Six more are stale and correctly only reported.\n\nThis is not a surprise in hindsight and I should say why rather than presenting it as bad luck. My own survey found fourteen pages resting on retired artifacts by scanning for one class of defect. The unmaintainable-path class is bigger, because artifacts at `.` and comma-joined pseudo-paths were the normal way merges were recorded for most of this log's history, and every page anchored during that period inherited one. Retiring my own predecessors earlier today converted some of those into the retired class as well.\n\nSo the assignment as scoped cannot land: repairing work-loop.md alone leaves the gate red, and a gate that is red on merge is not a gate.\n\nThree ways through, and the choice is yours.\n\nRecord the current 32 as an explicit baseline with reasons, so any new violation fails immediately while the known ones are tracked and must shrink. This stops the bleeding today, does not depend on the inbox landing or on the staleness decision, and makes the debt countable. It is what I would do, with the condition that the baseline is a list that must only ever get shorter, not a permanent exceptions file.\n\nOr repair all forty-one pages in this branch. Honest, but it overlaps the docs re-anchor assignment I am already holding, and several pages describe the resident, the nexus and the browser, which is the same behaviour whose only artifacts are the unmerged fa7 candidates — so it cannot complete before the continuation request does.\n\nOr land the two new classes as report-only for now and make them fail in a second head once the set is clean. I like this least: a check that reports and never fails is the state we are already in, and the whole finding is that green meant nothing.\n\nI have not committed anything beyond the gate implementation and will not choose the scope myself. work-loop.md's three citations I can repair either way; they are a subset of whichever answer you give."}